A1. If Senators didn't do this they'd probably never have anything to say. ~ ARISE
A7. What a dead-beat is always looking for. ~ TRUST
A12. The fountain of youth. ~ SODA
A13. Boarding house symbol. ~ PRUNE
A15. That janitor complexion. ~ ASHY
A16. This is always on the up and up. ~ HILL
A17. The original Aimee. ~ AMY
A18. To gather in. ~ REAP
A19. What coon-shouters shout for. ~ ELEVEN
A21. What husbands do with their watches after payday. ~ PLEDGE
A22. Living and breeding. ~ ALIVE
A24. The head that rocks the boat. ~ SAP
A26. The day of days. ~ PAY
A29. A kind of looking glass. ~ SPY
A31. To park. ~ PET
A33. The words a woman never says. ~ FEW
A34. Prominent club men. ~ POLICE
A35. Motion picture passion. ~ ANIMAL
A36. Spanish import which evades the customs, altho they have been known to catch it. ~ FLU
A37. This is short and deadly. ~ ASP
A39. This takes a lot of socks in a lifetime. ~ TOE
A40. If we didn't do this we'd all be threadbare. ~ SEW
A42. Some men will even do this for gold. ~ DIG
A45. If business men do this they'll never have to have conferences. ~ AGREE
A47. This is a future shadow builder. ~ ECLAIR
A50. Where mice breed in Scotland. ~ WALLET
A53. The "say when" line. ~ BRIM
A54. Boys will be boys if they are this. ~ BAD
A56. No fish in this sea. ~ LAVA
A57. They hold this in fast company. ~ RACE
A58. One of these a day will keep the blues away—if you have the gin. ~ LEMON
A59. The turn motorists should avoid. ~ OVER
A60. This fellow is forever introducing something. ~ AGENT
A61. How you can always find the hired man. ~ INERT

D1. Rug preservers. ~ ASHES
D2. Try this on a lion and you'll hear from him. ~ ROIL
D3. No beer and no work. ~ IDLE
D4. Soft soaps. ~ SALVES
D5. The wife of Zeus beheaded. ~ ERA
D6. The man spinsters want. ~ ANY
D8. This is when a woman admits she is wrong. ~ RARELY
D9. The car that came back. ~ USED
D10. Put this in your pipe and choke on it. ~ SHAG
D11. What a stenographer does while she yearns for the boss son. ~ TYPES
D14. A base robber. ~ UMPIRE
D20. The busy executive's afternoon conference. ~ NAP
D21. A self-starter. ~ PEP
D23. The place Adam hung his collar. ~ APPLE
D25. Lay your troubles here. ~ ASIDE
D27. What a man brings his wife at four a. m. ~ ALIBI
D28. The kindest cut of all. ~ MELON
D30. Here's looking at you. ~ YOU
D31. This is forever in the soup. ~ PEA
D32. A splendid opening for a young college man. ~ TAP
D33. The kind of shadow that follows Al Capone. ~ FAT
D38. A soprano in full blast. ~ SCREAM
D40. Ahoy polloi! ~ SEAMEN
D41. This is all quiet on the western front. ~ WAR
D42. The reverse of married. ~ DEW
D43. As this goes so does the party. ~ GALLON
D44. Sport model jackass. ~ ZEBRA
D46. An Irishman's part in a fight. ~ START
D48. Steep, rugged rock. ~ CRAG
D49. Faithful warworkers on the western front. ~ LICE
D51. How to keep your neck clean in Boston. ~ LAVE
D52. What atheists will die for. ~ EVER
D54. Don't let anyone put this on you. ~ BEE
D55. When he puts on a collar he's all dressed up. ~ DOG


